      "notes": "Loop-prevention: skips processing if the sender IS the tenant's own connected mailbox (e.g. Elliot's own sent replies landing back in a shared/CC'd inbox). TODO: verify this is sufficient in your actual Gmail setup -- if the mailbox is a shared alias, you may need a header-based marker instead (e.g. a custom X-Elliot-Sent header stamped by 15) for a more reliable check."
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "operation": "executeQuery",
        "query": "insert into contacts (tenant_id, email)\nvalues ($1, $2)\non conflict (tenant_id, email)\ndo update set email = excluded.email\nreturning *;",
        "options": {
          "queryReplacement": "={{ [$('Lookup Tenant by Mailbox').first().json.tenant_id, $('Extract Email Fields').first().json.from_email] }}"
        }
      },
      "id": "ef564099-e992-4b7d-8326-2c9e9d7e6813",
      "name": "Upsert Contact",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.postgres",
      "typeVersion": 2.5,
      "position": [
        -16,
        -32
      ],
      "credentials": {
        "postgres": {
          "id": "TfAL8Z0epZ1Tqiib",
          "name": "Elliot Postgres (service role)"
        }
      },
      "notes": "Upserts on the tenant_id+email unique constraint from Phase 5. Deliberately no lead scoring/escalation here -- this just makes sure an email from a new sender gets a contact row, same as chat does via capture_lead, so email leads don't fall outside the pipeline. TODO: confirm 'contacts' table has no other NOT NULL columns beyond tenant_id/email that would make a bare insert fail (e.g. a required 'source' or 'name' column) -- add them to the insert if so."
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "operation": "executeQuery",
        "query": "-- Replaces the current query in \"Get or Create Conversation (Email)\".\n-- Old version only returned a row on first creation -- returned nothing if\n-- the conversation already existed, which is fine when nothing downstream\n-- needs the ID, but message-logging needs a reliable ID every time.\nwith existing as (\n  select id from conversations\n  where tenant_id = $1 and contact_id = $2 and channel = 'email'\n  limit 1\n),\ninserted as (\n  insert into conversations (tenant_id, contact_id, channel)\n  select $1, $2, 'email'\n  where not exists (select 1 from existing)\n  returning id\n)\nselect id from existing\nunion all\nselect id from inserted;",
        "options": {
          "queryReplacement": "={{ [$('Lookup Tenant by Mailbox').first().json.tenant_id, $json.id] }}"
        }
      },
      "id": "9dd81ec1-a4d8-46fd-b524-e84e9f1f2fee",
      "name": "Get or Create Conversation (Email)",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.postgres",
      "typeVersion": 2.5,
      "position": [
        32,
        224
      ],
      "credentials": {
        "postgres": {
          "id": "TfAL8Z0epZ1Tqiib",
          "name": "Elliot Postgres (service role)"
        }
      },
      "notes": "TODO: this INSERT...WHERE NOT EXISTS returns nothing on the 'already exists' path (a Postgres quirk) -- you'll need a follow-up SELECT (or a CTE combining both) to reliably get the conversation_id either way. Also confirm 'conversations' table actually has a 'channel' column -- if it doesn't, this needs a small migration too, and email threads should probably map many gmail_thread_ids to one ongoing conversation per contact rather than one-per-thread; adjust the matching logic if so."
    },
